      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Current law permits abortion up to the point of fetal viability. The proposed measure overrides this by prohibiting abortions after 15 weeks of gestation, limiting exceptions strictly to cases where the pregnant woman's life is at risk or to prevent serious, irreversible physical impairment of a major bodily function.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "The costs and burdens are borne by pregnant individuals who seek abortions after 15 weeks but do not meet the narrow medical exceptions, as well as by medical providers whose legal liability increases. The policy benefits fetuses beyond 15 weeks of gestation and individuals who advocate for stricter legal limits on abortion.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Individuals seeking an abortion after 15 weeks are likely to travel to other states where the procedure remains legal, seek methods outside the formal healthcare system, or carry pregnancies to term. Physicians may alter their practices, potentially hesitating or delaying care in complex medical situations near the 15-week mark to ensure compliance with the strict emergency exceptions.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The text addresses the legality of abortions occurring after 15 weeks by directly prohibiting them. This mechanism strictly limits access but may create perverse incentives in the healthcare system, where doctors might delay medically necessary interventions until a patient's condition deteriorates sufficiently to clearly meet the narrow legal exceptions for life or major bodily impairment.",
      "disputed": false
    }
